1
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:11,660
A Place of Our Own

2
00:00:11,660 --> 00:00:14,800
Ellen, I'd like your opinion.

3
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:17,000
About what?

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:23,010
Well, Richard and I feel that with a baby coming

5
00:00:23,010 --> 00:00:30,260
we need to have our own place to live.

6
00:00:30,260 --> 00:00:31,470
Oh.

7
00:00:31,470 --> 00:00:32,970
Well, what do you think about Richard and me

8
00:00:32,970 --> 00:00:35,720
looking for a small house or an apartment

9
00:00:35,720 --> 00:00:39,550
at this point in our lives?

10
00:00:39,550 --> 00:00:41,620
We love having you here,

11
00:00:41,620 --> 00:00:42,710
and there is room,

12
00:00:42,710 --> 00:00:45,490
and ... and when the baby comes,

13
00:00:45,490 --> 00:00:49,680
the baby can stay in your room for a while.

14
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:57,330
Richard feels we need to find a small house.

15
00:00:57,330 --> 00:01:02,250
I remember when I was pregnant with Richard.

16
00:01:02,250 --> 00:01:04,000
Philip and I were living with Grandma and Grandpa.

17
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:05,100
Philip was a young doctor,

18
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:09,870
and he kept talking about having a house of our own.

19
00:01:09,870 --> 00:01:11,470
It's natural.

20
00:01:11,470 --> 00:01:13,760
What did you do?

21
00:01:13,760 --> 00:01:16,960
We looked at a lot of houses.

22
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:18,630
Did you find one?

23
00:01:18,630 --> 00:01:19,510
Oh, not at first.

24
00:01:19,510 --> 00:01:21,200
We couldn't afford it.

25
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:26,020
Grandpa wanted to lend us the money to buy one,

26
00:01:26,020 --> 00:01:28,120
but Philip is too independent.

27
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:31,660
He didn't want to borrow any money.

28
00:01:31,660 --> 00:01:33,400
Sounds like Richard.

29
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:35,110
They're all alike.

30
00:01:35,110 --> 00:01:39,020
Richard is a real Stewart.

31
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:39,920
He's independent,

32
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:46,030
and sometimes just stubborn.

33
00:01:46,030 --> 00:01:48,580
When did you buy a house?

34
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:51,790
After Richard was born.

35
00:01:51,790 --> 00:01:52,740
I was teaching music,

36
00:01:52,740 --> 00:01:57,650
and Philip was opening his first medical office.

37
00:01:57,650 --> 00:01:58,840
Where was the house?

38
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:00,830
Right here in Riverdale.

39
00:02:00,830 --> 00:02:04,990
Of course, it was a small house,

40
00:02:04,990 --> 00:02:08,610
but just right for us.

41
00:02:08,610 --> 00:02:11,050
It's funny. History repeats itself.

42
00:02:11,050 --> 00:02:12,660
Now Richard and I are having a baby,

43
00:02:12,660 --> 00:02:14,220
and we probably won't be able to

44
00:02:14,220 --> 00:02:16,900
afford a house right away, either.

45
00:02:16,900 --> 00:02:20,890
Why don't you look at some houses, Marilyn?

46
00:02:20,890 --> 00:02:23,050
Good idea. \u597D\u4E3B\u610F\u3002

47
00:02:23,050 --> 00:02:26,990
Look in the real-estate section of Sunday's Times.

48
00:02:26,990 --> 00:02:28,360
You'll learn a lot.

49
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:30,120
Maybe we should

50
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:33,060
speak to a real-estate agent about a house.

51
00:02:33,060 --> 00:02:35,900
And a bank about a mortgage.

52
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:38,130
I'll talk to Richard about it.

53
00:02:38,130 --> 00:02:39,490
I think it's a good idea, Ellen.

54
00:02:39,490 --> 00:02:42,740
We can learn a lot by asking.

55
00:02:42,740 --> 00:02:44,990
And if I can be of any help,

56
00:02:44,990 --> 00:02:45,960
let me know.

57
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:46,990
As a matter of fact,

58
00:02:46,990 --> 00:02:49,970
my friend Virginia Martinelli is a real-estate agent.

59
00:02:49,970 --> 00:02:51,170
Good.

60
00:02:51,170 --> 00:02:52,450
You won't believe this,

61
00:02:52,450 --> 00:02:56,990
but she sold us our first house and this one.

62
00:02:56,990 --> 00:02:57,710
Well, I'll tell Richard,

63
00:02:57,710 --> 00:03:06,210
and we'll go to see her.

64
00:03:06,210 --> 00:03:10,520
Do you think the skirt length is right, Ellen?

65
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:14,020
Do you think it's too long?

66
00:03:14,020 --> 00:03:16,830
I think the skirt is just right.

67
00:03:16,830 --> 00:03:19,350
Are you planning to attach a train to it?

68
00:03:19,350 --> 00:03:23,190
No. No train. Just the dress.

69
00:03:23,190 --> 00:03:27,270
But I am going to make a headpiece of lace.

70
00:03:27,270 --> 00:03:30,300
That dress is gorgeous.

71
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:32,290
Thanks, Ellen.

72
00:03:32,290 --> 00:03:35,640
And thanks for the advice about the house.

73
00:03:35,640 --> 00:03:38,090
I'll talk to Richard about it

74
00:03:38,090 --> 00:03:38,860
the minute he comes home.

75
00:03:38,860 --> 00:03:40,520
And remember,

76
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:43,430
we love having you here.

77
00:03:43,430 --> 00:03:46,430
There's no need to rush.
